WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.850
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.990
I have recently been witnessing a few heated
conversations regarding the submission of

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.059
deep learning papers to computer vision conferences.

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:18.779
The forums are up in arms about the fact that
despite some of these papers showcased remarkably

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:23.789
good results, they were rejected on the basis
of, from what I have heard, not adding too

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:28.570
much to the tree of knowledge. They argue
that we don't understand what is going on

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:32.500
in these neural networks and cannot really
learn anything new from them.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.809
I'll try to understand and rephrase their
argument differently. We know exactly how

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:43.280
to train a neural network, it's just that
as an output of this process, we get a model

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.370
of something that resembles a brain as a collection
of neurons and circumstances under which these

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:55.449
neurons are activated. We store these in a
file that can take up to several gigabytes,

00:00:55.449 --> 00:00:59.929
and the best solutions are often not intuitively
understandable for us.

00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:04.519
For instance, in this video, we're training
a neural network to classify these points

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:09.630
correctly, but what exactly can we learn if
we look into these neurons? Now imagine that

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:14.850
in practice, we don't have a handful of these
boxes, but millions of them, and more complex

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.509
than the ones you see here.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:21.799
Let's start with a simple example that hopefully
helps getting a better grip of this argument.

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:26.329
Now, I'll be damned if this video won't be
more than a couple minutes, so this is going

00:01:26.329 --> 00:01:31.619
to be one of those slightly extended Two Minute
Paper episodes. I hope you don't mind!

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:37.850
The grammatical rules of my native language,
a lot of them, are contained in enormous tomes

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:43.420
that everyone has to go through during their
school years. Rules are important. They give

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.960
the scaffoldings for constructing sentences
that are grammatically correct.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:53.270
Can we explain or even enumerate these rules?
Well, unless you are a linguist, the answer

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.340
is no.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:59.380
Almost no one really remembers more than a
few rules, but every native speaker knows

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.520
how their language should be spoken. And it
is because we've heard a lot of sentences

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.569
that are correct and learned it by heart what
makes a proper sentence and what is gibberish.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:16.159
This is exactly what neural networks do - they
are trained in a very similar way. In fact,

00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:21.299
they are so effective at it that if we you
try to forcefully insert some of our knowledge

00:02:21.299 --> 00:02:24.090
in there, the solutions are going to get worse.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:29.720
It is therefore an appropriate time to ask
questions like what merits a paper and what

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.290
do we define as scientific progress. What
if we have extremely accurate algorithms where

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.700
we don't know what is going on under the hood,
or simpler, more intuitive algorithms that

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:46.930
may be subpar in accuracy. If we have a top
tier scientific conference where only a very

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.930
limited number of papers get accepted, which
ones shall we accept?

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:56.159
I hope that this question will spark a productive
discussion, and hopefully scientific research

00:02:56.159 --> 00:02:59.650
venues will be more vigilant about this question
in the future.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:06.750
Okay, so the question is crystal clear: knowledge
or efficiency? How about possible solutions?

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:11.150
Can we extract scientific insights out of
these neural networks?

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.549
Model compression is a way to essentially
compress the information in this brain-ish

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:18.620
thing, this collection of neurons we described
earlier.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:24.870
To demonstrate why this is such a cool idea,
let's quickly jump to this program by DeepMind

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:30.920
that plays Atari games at an amazingly high
level. In breakout, the solution program that

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.420
you see here is essentially an enormous table
that describes what the program should do

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:42.549
when it sees different inputs. It is so enormous
that it has many millions of records in there.

00:03:42.549 --> 00:03:48.469
A manual of many thousand pages, if you will.
It is easy to execute for a computer, but

00:03:48.469 --> 00:03:53.180
completely impossible for us to understand
why and how it works.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:58.629
However, if we intuitively think about the
game itself, we could actually write a super

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:04.530
simple program in one line of code that would
almost be as good as this. All we need to

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:11.530
do is try to follow the ball with the paddle.
One line of code, and pretty decent results.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:17.050
Not optimal, but quite decent. From such a
program, we can actually learn something about

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.260
the game.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:23.060
Essentially what we could do with these enormous
tables, is compressing them into much much

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.940
smaller ones. Ones that are so tiny, that
we can actually build an intuition from them.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:34.470
This way, the output of a machine learning
technique wouldn't only be an extremely efficient

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:40.950
program, but finally, the output of the procedure
would be knowledge. Insight. If you think

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:47.140
about it, such an algorithm would essentially
do research by itself. At first, it would

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:52.700
randomly try experimenting, and after a large
amount of observations are collected, these

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:59.350
observations would be explained by a small
number of rules. That is exactly the definition

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:00.760
of research.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:06.200
And perhaps, this is one of the more interesting
future frontiers of machine learning research.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:12.220
And by the way, earlier we have talked about
a fantastic paper on Neural Programmer Interpreters

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:18.130
that also aimed to output complete algorithms
that can be directly used and understood.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.190
The link is available in the description box.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:24.170
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

